# q38-27b-mxfp4.recipe -- Qwen3.8-27B converted from its bf16 checkpoint, as the q38-27b-mxfp4.rad
# container serves it: an OCP-MXFP4 trunk under fp8 activations, a block-fp8 MTP head and DFlash2
# drafter, everything else as the checkpoint holds it.
#
#   rad-convert <Qwen3.8-27B bf16 checkpoint> --quant-id mxfp4 \
#       --recipe data/recipes/q38-27b-mxfp4.recipe -o q38-27b-mxfp4.rad
#
#   # the same, with the DFlash2 drafter in place of the MTP head:
#   rad-convert <Qwen3.8-27B bf16 checkpoint> --draft-model <DFlash2 checkpoint> \
#       --quant-id mxfp4 --recipe data/recipes/q38-27b-mxfp4.recipe -o q38-27b-mxfp4-df2.rad
#
# The checkpoint carries no quantization_config, so everything a rule does not name stays bf16: the
# norms and biases, A_log, dt_bias, conv1d, the GDN in_proj_a/in_proj_b (48 rows each, not a whole
# group of 32), the router -- and the vision tower (v.*), which libr4d serves in bf16. Rank-1
# weights a pattern happens to match are declined by the quantiser and kept as they are. The first
# rule that matches a weight decides it.
#
# The trunk linears -- attention q/k/v/o, the delta net's fused in_proj_qkv|in_proj_z and out_proj,
# the MLP gate_up and down -- are OCP MXFP4: E2M1 codes with an E8M0 shared exponent a group of 32,
# which the grid spells `codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0` (and e8m0's own default rule is the
# OCP shared exponent, so `rule=mx` need not be written). libr4d reads that encoding as three views
# of one weight -- the packed e2m1 codes, the [K/32, N] exponent plane, and a per-row u8 reference
# the layout derives -- through its `gemm_mxfp4a8` rows; the activation is E4M3 with one f32 scale
# a row. The lm_head is block fp8, not MXFP4: the widest GEMM in the model at half the bytes.

# The token embedding, E4M3 under ONE f32 scale (`block=*x*`, read by embed_lookup_q): half the
# bytes of the bf16 table -- 1.18 GiB at 248k x 5120 -- and all of it handed to the KV cache. A
# gather reads a handful of rows a step, so the format costs no time. The arch reads the format off
# the container, so a container converted without this line keeps the bf16 table and embed_lookup.
token_embd.weight                    rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=*x* scale=f32 rule=search

# The lm_head, block fp8 -- NOT MXFP4, and not bf16. It is the widest GEMM in the model and the
# plan keeps it at block fp8 (half the bytes, and the logits path libr4d already ships). No other
# rule matches `output.weight`, so without this line it would silently stay bf16.
output.weight                        rtn  codes=i4 group=128 scale=bf16

# The MTP head, block fp8. Its DECODER-block linears reuse the fp8 blocks, and its one extra block
# is declared as trunk layer 64 (n_main), so those weights carry a `blk.64.` prefix and their fp8
# rule sits AHEAD of the trunk's MXFP4 rules. `mtp.fc.weight` is deliberately NOT quantised: the MTP
# block hard-codes it bf16 and reads it with the unquantised `gemm_nt` (rad_block_mtp_fp8.h), so a
# `mtp.*` rule would plan an fp8 grid under a gemm with no quantised row and the conversion would
# refuse. Its four norms are rank-1 and declined anyway.
mtp.draft_head.weight                rtn  codes=u2 zero=u8 group=128 scale=f16
# `64` is this checkpoint's trunk depth (num_hidden_layers): the MTP layer is declared at index
# n_main = 64. On a different member of the family this rule would land on a real trunk layer, so it
# is model-specific by construction.
blk.64.*.weight                      rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16

# The DFlash2 drafter's 2-bit copy of the target's head -- u2 codes, an f16 scale and a u8 zero a
# group of 128 -- read by its draft rounds only. A draft is verified by the trunk, so this moves
# acceptance and never an emitted token.
dflash.draft_head.weight             rtn  codes=u2 zero=u8 group=128 scale=f16

# The DFlash2 drafter's linears, block fp8 from its bf16 release: read whole on every decode step.
# This is the fp8 DEFAULT. The OCP-MXFP4 drafter variant (RADIANCE_MX_DF2_DRAFTER_MX, default off)
# is data/recipes/q38-27b-mxfp4-df2mx.recipe, which is this file with these six rules swapped for
# `rtn codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0`; build that container and serve it with the env flag set.
dflash.fc.weight                     rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_q.weight           rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_k.weight           rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_v.weight           rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_output.weight      rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.ffn_gate_up.weight      rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.ffn_down.weight         rtn  codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16

# The trunk linears, OCP MXFP4. The encoding a kernel reads is exactly the codes and the shared
# exponent -- no stored column scales -- so `rtn` is the form; AWQ's extra level is one the mxfp4
# layout does not select.
blk.*.attn_qg.weight                 rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
blk.*.attn_k.weight                  rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
blk.*.attn_v.weight                  rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
# The FUSED q|gate,k,v projection, declared only under RADIANCE_MX_ATTN_FUSE_QKV (default off; see
# rad_block_attn_gated_mxfp4.h). It is the q_proj, k_proj and v_proj checkpoint tensors joined along
# dim 0, and the block owns this ONE name when it fuses -- so a fused conversion applies this rule
# and the three above are simply never declared. The encoding is the same OCP MXFP4 as the trunk.
blk.*.attn_qkv.weight                rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
blk.*.attn_output.weight             rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
blk.*.ssm_inz.weight                 rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
blk.*.ssm_out.weight                 rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
blk.*.ffn_gate_up.weight             rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
blk.*.ffn_down.weight                rtn  codes=fp4_e2m1 group=32 scale=e8m0
